&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Portland, OR &mdash; &lt;/strong&gt;The Oregon Historical Society (OHS) is proud to present &ldquo;&lt;a href="https://www.ohs.org/blog/oregon-connections-a-series-on-the-right-to-be-free.cfm"&gt;Oregon Connections: A Conversation Series on the Right to Be Free&lt;/a&gt;,&rdquo; a free, all-virtual program series beginning January 29, 2026. Designed to coincide with the semiquincentennial (250th anniversary) of the Declaration of Independence, this innovative series brings together historians, legal scholars, and public audiences to explore how people in Oregon have engaged in the ongoing struggle for freedom and justice.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Spanning five sessions, the series highlights both landmark and lesser-known stories from Oregon&rsquo;s past that illuminate local and national conversations about rights such as freedom of speech, citizenship, due process, and community action. Each session brings together expert speakers and opens the virtual floor to audience questions and discussion.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&ldquo;Oregon&rsquo;s history shows us that the work of freedom is neither simple nor complete,&rdquo; said Eliza E. Canty-Jones, chief program officer at OHS. &ldquo;Through moments big and small &mdash; from Oregon&rsquo;s early racial exclusion laws to individual acts of resistance &mdash; this series invites us to listen, learn, and reflect on how people have defined and pursued the conditions necessary for liberty.&rdquo;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2026 Oregon Connections Series Schedule&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;em&gt;(All programs are virtual and take place at 12pm PT; &lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="https://orhs.ejoinme.org/OregonConnections"&gt;&lt;em&gt;register here&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt;)&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
     &lt;li&gt;January 29: &ldquo;&lt;a href="https://www.ohs.org/events/oregon-connections-incarceration.cfm"&gt;Incarceration&lt;/a&gt;&rdquo; with Peggy Nagae and Kimberly Jensen&lt;/li&gt;
     &lt;li&gt;February 12: &ldquo;&lt;a href="https://www.ohs.org/events/oregon-connections-immigration-and-deportation.cfm"&gt;Immigration and Deportation&lt;/a&gt;&rdquo; with Chelsea Rose and Jerry Garcia&lt;/li&gt;
     &lt;li&gt;March 19: &ldquo;&lt;a href="https://www.ohs.org/events/oregon-connections-tribal-sovereignty-and-civil-rights.cfm"&gt;Tribal Sovereignty and Civil Rights&lt;/a&gt;&rdquo; with Bobbie Conner and Robert Kentta&lt;/li&gt;
     &lt;li&gt;May 21: &ldquo;&lt;a href="https://www.ohs.org/events/oregon-connections-race-citizenship-and-labor.cfm"&gt;Race, Citizenship, and Labor&lt;/a&gt;&rdquo; with Jennifer Fang and Johanna Ogden&lt;/li&gt;
     &lt;li&gt;June 4: &ldquo;&lt;a href="https://www.ohs.org/events/oregon-connections-community-organizing.cfm"&gt;Community Organizing&lt;/a&gt;&rdquo; with Kimberly Jensen, Carmen Thompson, and Diane Hess&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;All conversations will be recorded and made available for later viewing on OHS&rsquo;s Past Programs page, along with associated resources to support further exploration of Oregon&rsquo;s rich and complex history.&lt;/p&gt;

